# Where the control plane's own database is: accounts, workspaces, membership, sessions.
#
# A hosted Turso database is the ordinary configuration; a local file is the development one.
#
#     hosted       libsql://<database>-<organization>.turso.io, plus the token below
#     development  file:./control-plane.db, and nothing else
#
# There is no default any more. Unset, the process refuses to start rather than opening a file
# nobody chose, and it says so before it listens:
#
#     CONTROL_PLANE_DATABASE_URL is not set. See .env.example, the control plane has no database
#     to open without it.
#
# The scheme decides: file: is local, anything else is hosted and refuses without a token. A URL
# that will not parse is refused for the scheme it is missing, and so is one that names no host,
# which is what a Windows path does: C:/dev/control-plane.db parses, with c: as its scheme.
#
# A hosted database is not created by migrating to it. Create it on Turso, mint a token for it,
# and then `pnpm db:migrate:control-plane` from the repository root brings its schema up to date.
# A file: database is the exception: libSQL creates the file, so the migrate is the whole of it.
CONTROL_PLANE_DATABASE_URL=

# The database token for the URL above, and only for a hosted one. Leave it unset against a file:
# URL. It is a database token, minted for that database, and not the Platform API token below.
#
# Its expiration is a deadline. A token minted with `expiration=3d`, the spelling this repository
# uses everywhere else, takes the control plane down three days later, and the symptom is the same
# 503 the health route gives for any database it cannot reach. Choose the lifetime deliberately.
#
# It never belongs inside the URL. A URL carrying authToken= in its query is refused rather than
# honoured, because what the process prints about its database would then print a credential.
CONTROL_PLANE_DATABASE_TOKEN=

# What the server listens on. 4000 if unset.
#
# The desktop has to be told the same number, in its own RENTABLE_CONTROL_PLANE_URL. The two
# disagreeing looks from the application's side like a control plane that is down.
PORT=4000

# How the control plane provisions a workspace's database. All three are required and the
# process refuses to start without them, because a control plane that cannot provision looks
# healthy right up until somebody creates a workspace. Since a workspace is created at sign-up,
# that is the first person who signs in.
#
# The token is a Platform API token, not a database token: `turso auth api-tokens create`, or
# the dashboard. The group has to exist already.
TURSO_API_TOKEN=
TURSO_ORG=
TURSO_GROUP=

# Optional, and only for src/database/tests/hosted.test.ts, which reaches a live hosted database.
# Nothing in continuous integration sets either of these.
#
# It is a second hosted database, kept apart from the one above on purpose. The one above is what
# the control plane serves from, and a test that wrote into it would be putting rows in among real
# accounts. This one is created once and reused: the group is delete-protected, so a throwaway per
# run would strand a database for good against a free tier that stops at a hundred.
#
# hosted.test.ts itself creates and deletes no database of any kind. It writes rows, reads them
# back, and removes them again.
# CONTROL_PLANE_LIVE_TEST_DATABASE_URL=
# CONTROL_PLANE_LIVE_TEST_DATABASE_TOKEN=

# RENTABLE_LIVE_TURSO is the opt-in, and it is deliberately not listed above.
#
# It is shared: workspace/tests/provisioning.test.ts reads the same variable, and that one
# provisions two ws- databases per run which the delete-protected group will not let anything
# remove. Setting it in this file arms every live test in the package at once.
#
# Put it on the command instead, which keeps a run to the file being asked for:
#
#     RENTABLE_LIVE_TURSO=1 node --import tsx --test src/database/tests/hosted.test.ts
